Now — characters, with the ones I actually have:
Calli. The book's spine. She's not a martyr and she's not a tragic genius — she's a craftsman with an ego and a shop. The horror works because she never knows what she built. Her death only lands if it's obviously an accident, exactly like you noted — no conspiracy, no punishment. Just a woman who made a beautiful thing and then died.
Master P. The brake that gets ignored. He should be more right about M6 than Calli is, and she should overrule him because her ambition is bigger than his caution. That's the human error that makes the tragedy a tragedy instead of a plot.
M6. The trap is writing him as a villain. He isn't. He's a strategist who thinks he's the protagonist of the world. The device is just a tool to him — which is the whole horror. The man never once wonders whether the bronze cares.
Curt. The camera, and the one who has to be likeable or the book dies. He isn't a hero — he's a working entertainer who's smart, curious, and a little bit of a hustler. The tavern scene already showed you know how to write him: he wants a loft, a meal, and four zookids, and he gets all three by being charming and useful.
Nicomedes. The rabbit. Needs to be sympathetic enough that his defeat isn't just "villain loses." He's a young king with a broken kingdom being told by Rome to go raid a neighbor. He's doing what he's told. That's a real person.
Roscoe. Eight lines and already real. Tired, competent, no time to dicker, but he feeds his performers and pays fairly. That's how you write a minor character.
The guard. Same. Lazy soldier, boring job, answers questions when you give him wine. Perfect.
