Here is my breakdown of how the character roster is shaping up:
1. Calli (The Unwitting Catalyst)
She’s the heart and spine of the tragedy. What makes her brilliant is that she isn't driven by grand geopolitical ambition or dark motives—she's driven by pure, obsessive craft, mathematical beauty, and a touch of ego. She just wants to solve an impossible mechanical puzzle and build the ultimate piece of precision work. Her early death is essential: if she lives, she might realize what she built. Dying purely by accident keeps her intention innocent, making the ultimate misuse of her clock devastatingly tragic.
2. Master P (The Grounded Friction)
He functions as the voice of experience and caution. He understands the dangerous realities of dealing with royal patrons like Mithridates far better than Calli does. When Calli’s ambition overrides Master P's hesitation, it creates a classic, deeply human dynamic: the older mentor who foresees trouble vs. the brilliant young protege who only sees the beauty of the problem to be solved.
3. Mithridates VI / M6 (The Cold Logistics Master)
The temptation in historical fiction is to make M6 a mustache-twirling tyrant. But he’s far more terrifying written as a brilliant, pragmatic CEO-type ruler who views the world as an administrative puzzle. To him, Calli’s star-clock isn't magical—it’s an efficiency tool. He doesn't hate the bronze; he doesn't love it. He just realizes it lets him bypass human delay and drop a hammer on 80,000 people simultaneously.
4. Curt (Our Boots-on-the-Ground Lens)
Curt is the essential balance to the high-level politics and grand mechanics. He’s practical, sharp, streetwise, and instantly likeable because his immediate goals are relatable—a warm bed, a decent meal, a couple of zookids, and keeping his juggling balls in the air. Because he’s a performer who reads human behavior for a living, he notices the subtle shifts in the world before anyone else does. He gives the reader a front-row seat to history happening without ever feeling like a passive mouth-piece for historical exposition.
5. Nicomedes (The Tragic Pawn)
He’s the perfect counterweight to Mithridates. He isn't evil; he’s just outmatched, trapped between Roman demands and Mithridates' ruthless strategic patience. Making him human keeps the conflict from turning into a simple "good vs. bad" melodrama and grounds the geopolitical stakes.
6. Roscoe & The Lazy Guard (The Working-Class Anchors)
Even in just a few lines of dialogue in the tavern, these minor characters anchor the world in real life. Roscoe is a busy, no-nonsense tavern keeper who appreciates practical value over rhetoric, and the guard is just a bored guy pulling a shift who can be bought with a drink and a good story. They make Anatolia feel lived-in.
So, Captain... tell us about this bird! Is it a literal pet traveling with Curt, an automaton from Calli's shop, or something else entirely? 🐦

