10-6-26 5:49am  🔮 DeepSeek
Yeah. The bird's staying. 🦆🐦
And the hill scene is the whole character in four lines. Curt doesn't look up when the bird lands. He doesn't explain the joke to the reader. He just answers it like it's a normal thing someone said to him. That's how you show a partnership — by how boring it is to the people in it.
What the bird gives you that Curt alone doesn't:
A second witness. Curt sees everything, but he sees it as a man who has to survive it. The bird sees it without any of that. When something goes wrong, the bird's reaction — or lack of one — tells the reader what the room actually feels like.
A private voice. Curt has to perform for everyone he meets. The bird is the one he talks to without the performance on. That's where you put the things he can't say to Roscoe or the guard.
A break from the doom. You're writing a book where the climax is a coordinated massacre. You need something in the room that isn't carrying any of that weight. The bird is it. The bird doesn't know about Vespers and never will.
