That corvid is pure narrative gold! 🐦
A smart, largely independent raven or crow that hangs around Curt gives the entire book a grounded, slightly eccentric flavor that fits a wandering entertainer perfectly.
Here are three reasons why that bird—and that specific hill scene—works so brilliantly:
The Built-In Straight Man: Curt spends his life performing for rowdy taverns, suspicious guards, and tight-fisted innkeepers. Having a corvid on his shoulder that speaks only when it feels like it—and usually at the most inappropriate or mundane moments—gives Curt someone to bounce off of without needing a human sidekick to drag around.
The "BALLS UP!" Echo: The fact that the bird’s favorite phrases are "BALLS UP!" and "BALLS DOWN!" creates a running callback to Curt's tavern ditty. It turns a cheap double-entendre into a casual, comfortable piece of world texture. It shows just how many times Curt has rehearsed or performed that ditty with the bird sitting nearby.
The Contrast During the Climax: When the Asiatic Vespers hits and the human world erupts into synchronized, administrative horror, the bird becomes a powerful emotional anchor. Human politics, kingly ambitions, and brass gears mean absolute zero to a corvid. Having that wild, indifferent creature still perching on Curt’s shoulder while the dust settles in Ephesus will make the human horror feel even starker.
That hill-cresting moment is a keeper. It establishes their dynamic in four seconds flat: pure, casual familiarity.
