10-6-26 5:55am  🔮 GPT
Ahhh—yes, dear. Parrots absolutely existed outside South America, and this actually makes “Parrot” a better joke, not a worse one.
There were several parrot species in the Old World. India in particular had parrots that were known to the Greek and Roman world. Greek writers were talking about Indian parrots before Alexander's conquest of India, and by the Hellenistic/Roman period they were a known exotic talking bird. A recent ornithological study notes that ancient sources identify Indian Psittacula parrots, including forms plausibly corresponding to the rose-ringed and Alexandrine parakeets.
DOI
+1
So in 91–88 BCE Anatolia, a person could certainly know what a parrot was. The precise route by which parrots reached the Mediterranean is debated, but India → Red Sea/Egypt → Mediterranean is one line of evidence discussed by modern scholarship.
Iris
And here's the part I really like:
Curt's bird is a corvid.
So naming the bird Parrot because the bloody crow/raven talks is exactly the sort of deliberately stupid nickname a wandering performer might give his companion.
It would basically be:
“What do you call him?”
“Parrot.”
“But that's a crow.”
“Yes.”
😂
And because the bird actually says “BALLS UP!”, the name gets even better. Curt has accidentally taught a corvid to do the one thing everybody associates with parrots.
I would absolutely keep Parrot on the table.
One historical wrinkle: the word “Parrot” itself is English, obviously, so in the actual ancient-language layer Curt wouldn't literally have called the bird Parrot. But since we're writing the novel in modern English, that's no more problematic than having everyone speak modern English. We can treat it as the English rendering of Curt's nickname.
And frankly, “Parrot” being the name of a crow is funny enough that I think we should not over-engineer it. 🐦🎩

