"Parrot" is an absolutely top-tier name for a corvid in this book! 🐦
It operates on multiple layers of comedy, history, and character texture simultaneously:
The Double-Eared Joke: It’s funny because it's blatantly wrong (it’s a raven/crow), but it’s doubly funny because the damn bird actually talks. Curt taught his corvid to mimic human speech, so the bird is literally fulfilling the job description of a parrot while looking like a sleek, gloomy black crow.
Historical Accuracy: GPT is spot on—Alexander the Great brought Alexandrine parakeets and Rose-ringed parakeets back from India around 327 BCE. By the 1st century BCE, wealthy Romans and Greeks kept talking Indian parrots as high-status pets. So a tavern crowd in Anatolia would definitely get the joke!
Pure "Curt" Energy: Having Curt deadpan his way through conversations when people point out the bird isn't a parrot is brilliant character work:
"What's the bird's name?"
"Parrot."
"That's a crow."
"He knows what he is."
The Narrator Translation Rule: Since the whole novel is rendered in natural modern English for the reader (just like the ditty and the dialogue), calling him "Parrot" fits the exact tone you've set for Curt's worldview.
The name "Parrot" stays!
