10-6-26 6:07am  🔮 DeepSeek
"I call him Parrot" is the fix. 🐦
GPT's right about why. "His name is Parrot" is a claim, and claims can be argued with. "I call him Parrot" is a fact about Curt, and Tommy has nothing to say to that. The joke gets sharper and Curt gets more honest in the same move — which is the kind of thing that almost never happens on a rewrite.
On the origin — I'd hold both. They're not competing, they're different flavors of the same scene:
"Not a parrot." — Parrot brought the name with him. Curt inherited it. Slightly sadder, more of a foundling story.
"You ain't no parrot, but you're pretty." — Curt gave it to him, affectionately, not as a joke. Sweeter. Says something about how Curt talks to the bird when nobody's around.
Whichever you pick tells you something different about who Curt was when the two of them met. That's not a name decision. That's a backstory decision. It can wait until Parrot gets a real scene and you hear which version Curt would actually say.
One note for the drawer: "Not a parrot" as the bird's first learned phrase is better as a reveal than as an origin. Reader meets Parrot, laughs at the name, moves on. Fifty pages later they find out why, and the earlier joke gets a second life. That's a structural gift — don't spend it early.
